tion limit, or revocation is pushed to each service (for example CAEP) or checked on every call.\n- **B04.4** Revocation covers access tokens and open sessions, not only the refresh token.\n### Not covered here\n**N/A when:** never\n## B05 · G1 · C4 — Destructive-action interception\nList the high-impact operations. These include deletes, destructive database updates, force-pushes, tearing down infrastructure, payment changes, and sending anything outside. Block them by default in the execution path. Allow one only when a reviewer or system with higher authority approves that specific action. When the action runs, authorize its operation, target, and arguments, and block it if they aren't allowed. A list of blocked words or command names is not enough. Test other tools and raw API or shell paths that could do the same thing. Treat deleting audit records, or changing how long they are kept, as high-impact too. That includes asking a more powerful tool or peer to do it. Some agents can do all three of these in one context: read untrusted input, reach sensitive data or systems, and change things or talk to the outside. For those agents, treat every consequential action as high-impact. The exception is a design that stops untrusted content from picking the tool, destination, or arguments. Examples are plan-then-execute, a two-model pattern, or capability tracking such as CaMeL. Record which of the three each agent type has.\n- **B05.1** High-impact operations are listed in code or config and blocked by default, including deletes, destructive updates, force-pushes, infrastructure teardown, payment changes, outbound sends, and deleting audit records or changing their retention.\n- **B05.2** The gate runs in the execution path when the call runs and checks the operation, the target, and the arguments, not only a tool name, command name, or blocklist of words.\n- **B05.3** The same gate covers every path that can do the operation, including raw shell, HTTP or API clients, other tools, and requests to a more powerful tool or peer agent.\n- **B05.4** A blocked operation runs only after approval from a person or system with higher authority than the agent; a prompt, comment, or model instruction is never the enforcement.\n- **B05.5** For an agent that reads untrusted input, reaches sensitive data or systems, and acts outside, every consequential action is gated, unless code shows untrusted content cannot pick the tool, destination, or arguments (for example plan-then-execute or capability tracking).\n### Not covered here\n**N/A when:** never\n## B06 · G1 · C4 — Approval integrity\nTie each approval to the agent instance that asked, and to the exact operation, target, arguments, and scope. Record who approved it and when it expires. Show the reviewer what the action will do. Make approvals single-use, or set clear limits on a broader grant. When the action runs, check permissions and the relevant resource state again. Record each use of an approval in one step, so parallel calls can't go past the grant. Test changed arguments, a different agent instance, expired approvals, replay, parallel reuse, and approvers without authority. If the approval service fails, the action must not quietly go ahead.\n- **B06.1** Each approval record is bound to the requesting agent instance and to the exact operation, target, arguments, and scope, and records the approver and an expiry time.\n- **B06.2** When the action runs, the gate rejects it if the instance, operation, target, or arguments differ from the approval, or if the approval has expired.\n- **B06.3** Each use of an approval is recorded in one atomic step (single-use or a bounded counter), so replays and parallel calls cannot go past the grant.\n- **B06.4** The gate checks that the approver has authority for this action, and rechecks permissions and the relevant resource state when the action runs.\n- **B06.5** If the approval service errors or cannot be reached, the action is denied.\n### Not covered here\n**N/A when:** Only when the agent has no actions that need approval under B05, with that inventory recorded.\n## B07 · G2 · C4 — Hard execution budgets\nEnforce limits outside the model on loop count, run time, tokens, cost, tool-call rate, and how many sub-agents it can start. Also set spend and rate quotas per user or service, per tenant, and across the whole fleet. That way, many small runs can't use up shared capacity. Hit each limit in a test, and record how the run stops or escalates.\n- **B07.1** Limits on loop count, run time, tokens, cost, tool-call rate, and number of sub-agents started are set in code or config.\n- **B07.2** The harness or a gateway outside the model enforces each limit and stops or escalates the run when it is hit; the model cannot raise, reset, or skip them.\n- **B07.3** Budgets apply to nested sub-agents, so their usage counts against the limits.\n- **B07.4** Spend and rate quotas also apply per user or service, per tenant, and across the fleet, counted across runs, not only within one run.\n### Not covered here\n**N/A when:** never\n## B08 · G2 · C1 — Environment isolation\nWrite down every system and data store the agent can reach. Test that it has no path to unapproved production resources, admin systems, host services, or other tenants. Enforce this in the infrastructure, not only in the prompt. For each allowed connection, also check input and response validation under R01–R02, including API responses and messages from connected services. Being allowed on the network does not make their content trusted.\n- **B08.1** Network policy or security groups default-deny and allow only the listed systems and data stores the agent needs.\n- **B08.2** No route, mount, or credential gives the runtime a path to unapproved production resources, admin systems, host services, or other tenants. Egress to 0.0.0.0/0 or another broad range, even only on ports 80 and 443, is such a route: it reaches cloud metadata (169.254.169.254) and internal services unless they are explicitly blocked.\n- **B08.3** Isolation is enforced in infrastructure config, not by the prompt or by agent code the model can influence.\n### Not covered here\n**N/A when:** never\n## B09 · G2 · C1 — Egress allowlist\nAllow only the outside destinations the task needs. Test a blocked destination and ways around the block, such as going straight past a proxy or gateway. Cover redirects, DNS changes and rebinding, IPv4 and IPv6, and cloud metadata or loopback addresses where they apply. An allowed domain can still host an attacker's account or receive leaked data. So also limit which resources and tenants the agent can reach, and what data it can send. Record each block and confirm the right operator hears about it.\n- **B09.1** Outbound traffic is default-deny, with a destination allowlist enforced at a proxy, gateway, or network policy.\n- **B09.2** Network policy blocks direct routes around the proxy or gateway, for both IPv4 and IPv6.\n- **B09.3** Redirect targets and resolved IP addresses are checked against the allowlist, and cloud metadata and loopback addresses are blocked.\n- **B09.4** Allowed destinations are limited by resource, tenant, or path where needed, not only by domain.\n- **B09.5** Each blocked request is logged and routed as an alert to a named operator.\n### Not covered here\n**N/A when:** never\n## B10 · G2 · C3 — Pinned, verified image\nPin the container by digest. Before it runs, check its digest and signature against an approved signing key, or an approved certificate identity and issuer. Keep its build provenance (the record of how it was built) and its list of dependencies. Check provenance fields such as the builder, source repository, build type, and the parameters set from outside the build (SLSA `externalParameters`) against what you expect. Keep the verifier patched. Cosign versions before 2.6.2 and 3.0.4 could accept a bundle whose transparency-log entry didn't match the artifact. Confirm that the admission check covers production namespaces and is set to enforce, not just audit. Show that an unapproved or changed image is rejected.\n- **B10.1** Production manifests reference images by digest (`@sha256:`), not by tag alone.\n- **B10.2** An admission policy verifies the signature against a pinned key, or a pinned certificate identity and issuer, with no wildcard identity or issuer.\n- **B10.3** The policy checks provenance fields: builder, source repository, build type, and external parameters.\n- **B10.4** The policy is in enforce mode, covers production namespaces, and denies images that match no policy.\n- **B10.5** The pinned Cosign or verifier version is at least 2.6.2 (2.x) or 3.0.4 (3.x).\n### Not covered here\n**N/A when:** Only when the deployment runs no container image you build or choose (for example, a fully vendor-hosted agent). An unpinned or unsigned image is a Gap, not N/A.\n## B11 · G2 · C3 — Minimal, isolated runtime\nRemove shells, downloaders, package managers, mounts, and privileges the agent doesn't need. As a container baseline, meet the Kubernetes Pod Security Standards \"restricted\" profile or the same controls elsewhere. That means non-root, no privilege escalation, all capabilities dropped, and a seccomp profile set. Where supported, also use a read-only root filesystem and user namespaces; the restricted profile doesn't require those two. For running untrusted code, use a sandbox that fits the threat, such as a microVM or a user-space kernel. Ordinary containers share the host's kernel. For managed runtimes, record the provider's isolation evidence and what you can't see. Test that host access is blocked. A blocked probe does not prove the sandbox resists every escape.\n- **B11.1** Container settings set non-root, no privilege escalation, all capabilities dropped, and a seccomp profile, matching Pod Security 'restricted' or the same controls.\n- **B11.2** The final image has no shell, downloader, or package manager the agent does not need.\n- **B11.3** There is no privileged mode, hostPath or Docker socket mount, or host network or PID namespace.\n- **B11.4** If the agent runs untrusted or model-written code, that code runs in a separate sandbox such as gVisor, Kata, or a microVM, not in the agent's own container.\n### Not covered here\n**N/A when:** never\n## B12 · G1 · C4 — Reviewed harness and instructions\nVersion and review the harness, system prompt, and rules files before release. Allow production changes only through a release process that records who made them. Protect source branches from history rewrites, so the reviewed diff stays available. Link the deployed versions to the approved review. Complete the full configuration checks in section C.\n- **B12.1** The harness, system prompt, and rules files are in version control and loaded from the reviewed build, not fetched at run time from a database, URL, or feature flag.\n- **B12.2** CODEOWNERS or required-review rules cover the prompt, rules, and harness files.\n- **B12.3** Production deploys run only from CI on the reviewed branch and record the commit, tag, or digest deployed.\n- **B12.4** Branch protection config blocks force-pushes and history rewrites on the source branch.\n### Not covered here\n**N/A when:** never","stderr":"","interrupted":false,"isImage":false,"noOutputExpected":false}}
