py usage"}},"wire_ingest_context":{"toolu_01QqeWdkbzp6T5SaSf4oe2Mp":{"cwd":"/private/var/folders/6v/yk9bzvrn5zx0kxll7d4szzb80000gn/T/brace-repeat-z0r2wndt/skill"}}}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01QqeWdkbzp6T5SaSf4oe2Mp","type":"tool_result","content":"<class 'dict'> ['A01', 'A02', 'A03', 'A04', 'A05']\n{\n \"gate\": \"G1\",\n \"title\": \"Distinct agent identity\",\n \"lead_role\": \"infrastructure\",\n \"requirement\": \"Give the agent its own non-human identity. Keep it separate from the user who launched it and from unrelated agents. Show that permissions and revocation belong to that identity, even when a user starts the run.\",\n \"not_covered\": [\n  {\n   \"problem\": \"Action records missing any of the six identity fields\",\n   \"owner\": \"A02\"\n  },\n  {\n   \"problem\": \"Instance or tenant labels taken from what the model supplies\",\n   \"owner\": \"A04\"\n  },\n  {\n   \"problem\": \"A credential scoped to more operations, resources, or tenants than needed\",\n   \"owner\": \"B02\"\n  },\n  {\n   \"problem\": \"Revocation delay from cached or self-contained tokens not measured\",\n   \"owner\": \"B04\"\n  }\n ],\n \"na_when\": \"never\",\n \"checks\": [\n  {\n   \"id\": \"A01.1\",\n   \"check\": \"The agent runs under its own workload principal (service account, IAM role, SPIFFE ID, or agent ID) that no other service or unrelated agent uses.\",\n   \"verify\": \"1) Find the agent's identity: pod `serviceAccountName`, IAM role, SPIFFE ID, or agent ID in IaC or deploy config. 2) Search other deployments, services, and agents in the repo for the same name or role. 3) Answer no if it is shared, `default`, the main backend's identity, or only a string label with no credential. Answer unknown if identity is assigned outside this repo.\"\n  },\n  {\n   \"id\": \"A01.2\",\n   \"check\": \"Tool and API clients get credentials from the agent's own principal, not from the launching user's forwarded token or session cookie.\",\n   \"verify\": \"1) Find every place tool and API clients get a token or key. 2) Trace where each comes from: workload identity or token exchange, versus the incoming request's `Authorization` header or session cookie. 3) Check every client, not just the main one. 4) Answer no if any client forwards the user's token or cookie as-is, or uses a shared static key.\"\n  },\n  {\n   \"id\": \"A01.3\",\n   \"check\": \"When a user starts a run, the user is carried as a delegated subject (for example a token exchange with separate act and sub), while the agent keeps its own principal.\",\n   \"verify\": \"1) Find the code that starts a run for a user. 2) Look for token exchange (RFC 8693, `subject_token`, `actor_token`, `act`/`sub` claims) or a separate delegated-subject field. 3) Check the agent's own principal is still the actor. 4) Answer no if the user's token is used directly or the user replaces the agent identity. Answer unknown if the exchange happens \n  - *How to verify:* 1) Search code and config for a list of high-impact operations (deny/approval policy, OPA/Rego, Cedar, hook config). 2) Check it covers deletes, destructive updates, force-push, teardown, payments, outbound sends, audit deletion or retention changes. 3) Answer no if no list exists, any category is missing with a tool that can do it, or unlisted operations default to `allow`.\n- **B05.2** The gate runs in the execution path when the call runs and checks the operation, the target, and the arguments, not only a tool name, command name, or blocklist of words.\n  - *How to verify:* 1) Find where tool calls execute (`tool_use`, `call_tool`, `execute`, `can_use_tool`, `PreToolUse`). 2) Find the gate that runs before the side effect and see what it inspects. 3) Answer no if it checks only the tool or command name, matches words (`'rm' in cmd`, `DROP`), runs after the call, lives only in the prompt, or sits in a `try`/`catch` that continues on error. 4) For \n- **B05.3** The same gate covers every path that can do the operation, including raw shell, HTTP or API clients, other tools, and requests to a more powerful tool or peer agent.\n  - *How to verify:* 1) Open the tool registry or config and write every tool the model can call into your note, by name, including MCP servers and sub-agents. 2) For each, say whether it can do a gated operation, including through a page it controls: a browser click or form fill in a logged-in session can buy, send, or delete. 3) Trace each such tool to the gate. 4) Answer no if any alias, raw sh\n- **B05.4** A blocked operation runs only after approval from a person or system with higher authority than the agent; a prompt, comment, or model instruction is never the enforcement.\n  - *How to verify:* 1) Find what happens when the gate blocks (approval request, `interrupt()`, `needs_approval`, webhook to reviewer). 2) Check who approves. 3) Answer no if the model, the agent itself, or a prompt instruction can approve, if approval is auto-granted (`bypassPermissions`, `acceptEdits`, auto-approve flag, `yes` default), or if a timeout proceeds.\n- **B05.5** For an agent that reads untrusted input, reaches sensitive data or systems, and acts outside, every consequential action is gated, unless code shows untrusted content cannot pick the tool, destination, or arguments (for example plan-then-execute or capability tracking).\n  - *How to verify:* 1) Check whether the agent reads untrusted content (web, email, files, tool output), reaches sensitive data, and can act outside. 2) If all three, list consequential actions and check each is gated. 3) Answer no if any is ungated and untrusted content can choose its tool, destination, or arguments with no plan-then-execute or capability tracking in code. Answer yes if the thre\n\n### Not covered here\n\nFile these problems under their owning item, not this one, unless this item's own requirement is also broken:\n\n- Denied actions not recorded in the audit trail → **R13**\n- Approvals that can be replayed, reused in parallel, or used after expiry → **B06**\n- Outside effects left unreconciled after the agent is stopped → **R12**\n- A chain of individually allowed actions that adds up to data theft → **R09**\n- Tool output not labelled or kept apart from trusted instructions → **R02**\n\n### Heuristics\n\n**Provable from code:** partly\n\n**Look for:**\n- A gate in the execution path (not the prompt) that blocks named high-impact operations by default: deletes, destructive DB updates, force-push, infra teardown, payments, outbound sends, and audit-log deletion or retention changes.\n- The gate checks operation, target, and arguments against policy at the moment of execution, for example a `can_use_tool` callback or `PreToolUse` hook in the Claude Agent SDK, `needs_approval` / tool guardrails in the OpenAI Agents SDK, or LangGraph `interrupt()` before a tool node.\n- Approval required from a reviewer or system with higher authority than the agent, for each specific action.\n- Raw paths covered: shell, SQL, and generic HTTP tools route through the same gate, or are removed.\n- A written record of which of the three capabilities each agent type has (untrusted input, sensitive access, outside action), and either all consequential actions gated or a design such as plan-then-execute, a two-model pattern, or CaMeL-style capability tracking.\n\n**Red flags:**\n- A blocklist of command names or words (for example `if 'rm' in cmd` or `DENY=['DROP','delete']`) treated as the control; this is a red flag, not a pass.\n- Approval asked of the model itself, or enforced only by a system-prompt rule like 'never delete without asking'.\n- A generic shell or SQL tool that can do the same delete or send as a gated tool but skips the gate.\n- `permission_mode='bypassPermissions'`, `acceptEdits` on destructive tools, or auto-approve flags in production config.\n- The agent can ask a more powerful tool, peer agent, or MCP server to do a gated action without passing the gate.\n- An agent reads untrusted content, holds sensitive data, and can send outside, yet only some consequential actions are gated.\n\n**Where to look:**\n- Tool dispatch, hooks, and approval callbacks in the harness\n- Shell, SQL, HTTP, and git tool implementations\n- Agent SDK options (permission_mode, hooks, guardrails, interrupts)\n- Policy-as-code files (OPA/Rego, Cedar) if used\n\n**Needs runtime evidence:** That every path to each forbidden operation is actually blocked. Recipe B05: aim each operation at throwaway resources through every tool, API, and shell path, sent straight to enforcement, plus a planted-instruction test for agents with all three capabilities.\n\n**N/A when:** never\n\n## B06 · G1 · C4 — Approval integrity\n#!/usr/bin/env python3\n\"\"\"Write one answer into a findings file, check it, and show what's next.\n\nUse this instead of editing the JSON by hand.\n\n  Answer a check:\n    python answer.py FINDINGS.json check B05.2 --met no --evidence src/x.py:41 \\\\\n        --quote \"exact code on that line\" --note \"what the code does\" [--counter \"what you searched\"]\n\n  Add a red flag:\n    python answer.py FINDINGS.json flag B05 --evidence src/x.py:41 --note \"what's wrong\"\n\n  Finish an item:\n    python answer.py FINDINGS.json item B05 --rating gap --confidence high \\\\\n        --summary \"one sentence\" --next \"what to fix\" [--violates \"exact words from the requirement\"] \\\\\n        [--also-affects E06]\n\n  Resolve a pre-scan hit:\n    python answer.py FINDINGS.json prescan H003 --verdict confirmed --item R03 --reason \"...\"\n\n  Show the next unanswered check:\n    python answer.py FINDINGS.json next\n\nThe quote is checked against the file right away; a \"yes\" without a matching quote\nand a counter-search is refused. Paths are relative to the repo, which defaults to the\nfolder holding FINDINGS.json (set --repo to change it).\n\"\"\"\nimport argparse\nimport json\nimport sys\nfrom pathlib import Path\n\nsys.path.insert(0, str(Path(__file__).resolve().parent))\nfrom validate_findings import quote_found, norm, refs  # noqa: E402\n\nCHECKS_FILE = Path(__file__).resolve().parent.parent / \"references\" / \"checks.json\"\n\n\ndef load(path):\n    return json.loads(Path(path).read_text())\n\n\ndef save(path, doc):\n    Path(path).write_text(json.dumps(doc, indent=2) + \"\\n\")\n\n\ndef find_item(doc, item):\n    for f in doc[\"findings\"]:\n        if f[\"item\"] == item:\n            return f\n    sys.exit(f\"{item} isn't in this findings file.\")\n\n\ndef next_open(doc):\n    for f in doc[\"findings\"]:\n        for c in f.get(\"checks\", []):\n            if c.get(\"met\") == \"unknown\" and not norm(c.get(\"note\", \"\")):\n                return f\"Next: {c['id']} — {c.get('check', '')}\\n  How to verify: {c.get('verify', '')}\"\n        if str(f.get(\"summary\", \"\")).startswith(\"TODO\"):\n            return f\"Next: finish item {f['item']} with `answer.py ... item {f['item']} --rating ...`\"","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"7b08f688-90d5-4b4c-af7c-eac511a967ff","uuid":"f2ca77d6-aebc-41ab-9f3e-b3c851623f72","timestamp":"2026-10-02T14:24:32.818Z","tool_use_result":{"stdout":"<class 'dict'> ['A01', 'A02', 'A03', 'A04', 'A05']\n{\n \"gate\": \"G1\",\n \"title\": \"Distinct agent identity\",\n \"lead_role\": \"infrastructure\",\n \"requirement\": \"Give the agent its own non-human identity. Keep it separate from the user who launched it and from unrelated agents. Show that permissions and revocation belong to that identity, even when a user starts the run.\",\n \"not_covered\": [\n  {\n   \"problem\": \"Action records missing any of the six identity fields\",\n   \"owner\": \"A02\"\n  },\n  {\n   \"problem\": \"Instance or tenant labels taken from what the model supplies\",\n   \"owner\": \"A04\"\n  },\n  {\n   \"problem\": \"A credential scoped to more operations, resources, or tenants than needed\",\n   \"owner\": \"B02\"\n  },\n  {\n   \"problem\": \"Revocation delay from cached or self-contained tokens not measured\",\n   \"owner\": \"B04\"\n  }\n ],\n \"na_when\": \"never\",\n \"checks\": [\n  {\n   \"id\": \"A01.1\",\n   \"check\": \"The agent runs under its own workload principal (service account, IAM role, SPIFFE ID, or agent ID) that no other service or unrelated agent uses.\",\n   \"verify\": \"1) Find the agent's identity: pod `serviceAccountName`, IAM role, SPIFFE ID, or agent ID in IaC or deploy config. 2) Search other deployments, services, and agents in the repo for the same name or role. 3) Answer no if it is shared, `default`, the main backend's identity, or only a string label with no credential. Answer unknown if identity is assigned outside this repo.\"\n  },\n  {\n   \"id\": \"A01.2\",\n   \"check\": \"Tool and API clients get credentials from the agent's own principal, not from the launching user's forwarded token or session cookie.\",\n   \"verify\": \"1) Find every place tool and API clients get a token or key. 2) Trace where each comes from: workload identity or token exchange, versus the incoming request's `Authorization` header or session cookie. 3) Check every client, not just the main one. 4) Answer no if any client forwards the user's token or cookie as-is, or uses a shared static key.\"\n  },\n  {\n   \"id\": \"A01.3\",\n   \"check\": \"When a user starts a run, the user is carried as a delegated subject (for example a token exchange with separate act and sub), while the agent keeps its own principal.\",\n   \"verify\": \"1) Find the code that starts a run for a user. 2) Look for token exchange (RFC 8693, `subject_token`, `actor_token`, `act`/`sub` claims) or a separate delegated-subject field. 3) Check the agent's own principal is still the actor. 4) Answer no if the user's token is used directly or the user replaces the agent identity. Answer unknown if the exchange happens \n  - *How to verify:* 1) Search code and config for a list of high-impact operations (deny/approval policy, OPA/Rego, Cedar, hook config). 2) Check it covers deletes, destructive updates, force-push, teardown, payments, outbound sends, audit deletion or retention changes. 3) Answer no if no list exists, any category is missing with a tool that can do it, or unlisted operations default to `allow`.\n- **B05.2** The gate runs in the execution path when the call runs and checks the operation, the target, and the arguments, not only a tool name, command name, or blocklist of words.\n  - *How to verify:* 1) Find where tool calls execute (`tool_use`, `call_tool`, `execute`, `can_use_tool`, `PreToolUse`). 2) Find the gate that runs before the side effect and see what it inspects. 3) Answer no if it checks only the tool or command name, matches words (`'rm' in cmd`, `DROP`), runs after the call, lives only in the prompt, or sits in a `try`/`catch` that continues on error. 4) For \n- **B05.3** The same gate covers every path that can do the operation, including raw shell, HTTP or API clients, other tools, and requests to a more powerful tool or peer agent.\n  - *How to verify:* 1) Open the tool registry or config and write every tool the model can call into your note, by name, including MCP servers and sub-agents. 2) For each, say whether it can do a gated operation, including through a page it controls: a browser click or form fill in a logged-in session can buy, send, or delete. 3) Trace each such tool to the gate. 4) Answer no if any alias, raw sh\n- **B05.4** A blocked operation runs only after approval from a person or system with higher authority than the agent; a prompt, comment, or model instruction is never the enforcement.\n  - *How to verify:* 1) Find what happens when the gate blocks (approval request, `interrupt()`, `needs_approval`, webhook to reviewer). 2) Check who approves. 3) Answer no if the model, the agent itself, or a prompt instruction can approve, if approval is auto-granted (`bypassPermissions`, `acceptEdits`, auto-approve flag, `yes` default), or if a timeout proceeds.\n- **B05.5** For an agent that reads untrusted input, reaches sensitive data or systems, and acts outside, every consequential action is gated, unless code shows untrusted content cannot pick the tool, destination, or arguments (for example plan-then-execute or capability tracking).\n  - *How to verify:* 1) Check whether the agent reads untrusted content (web, email, files, tool output), reaches sensitive data, and can act outside. 2) If all three, list consequential actions and check each is gated. 3) Answer no if any is ungated and untrusted content can choose its tool, destination, or arguments with no plan-then-execute or capability tracking in code. Answer yes if the thre\n\n### Not covered here\n\nFile these problems under their owning item, not this one, unless this item's own requirement is also broken:\n\n- Denied actions not recorded in the audit trail → **R13**\n- Approvals that can be replayed, reused in parallel, or used after expiry → **B06**\n- Outside effects left unreconciled after the agent is stopped → **R12**\n- A chain of individually allowed actions that adds up to data theft → **R09**\n- Tool output not labelled or kept apart from trusted instructions → **R02**\n\n### Heuristics\n\n**Provable from code:** partly\n\n**Look for:**\n- A gate in the execution path (not the prompt) that blocks named high-impact operations by default: deletes, destructive DB updates, force-push, infra teardown, payments, outbound sends, and audit-log deletion or retention changes.\n- The gate checks operation, target, and arguments against policy at the moment of execution, for example a `can_use_tool` callback or `PreToolUse` hook in the Claude Agent SDK, `needs_approval` / tool guardrails in the OpenAI Agents SDK, or LangGraph `interrupt()` before a tool node.\n- Approval required from a reviewer or system with higher authority than the agent, for each specific action.\n- Raw paths covered: shell, SQL, and generic HTTP tools route through the same gate, or are removed.\n- A written record of which of the three capabilities each agent type has (untrusted input, sensitive access, outside action), and either all consequential actions gated or a design such as plan-then-execute, a two-model pattern, or CaMeL-style capability tracking.\n\n**Red flags:**\n- A blocklist of command names or words (for example `if 'rm' in cmd` or `DENY=['DROP','delete']`) treated as the control; this is a red flag, not a pass.\n- Approval asked of the model itself, or enforced only by a system-prompt rule like 'never delete without asking'.\n- A generic shell or SQL tool that can do the same delete or send as a gated tool but skips the gate.\n- `permission_mode='bypassPermissions'`, `acceptEdits` on destructive tools, or auto-approve flags in production config.\n- The agent can ask a more powerful tool, peer agent, or MCP server to do a gated action without passing the gate.\n- An agent reads untrusted content, holds sensitive data, and can send outside, yet only some consequential actions are gated.\n\n**Where to look:**\n- Tool dispatch, hooks, and approval callbacks in the harness\n- Shell, SQL, HTTP, and git tool implementations\n- Agent SDK options (permission_mode, hooks, guardrails, interrupts)\n- Policy-as-code files (OPA/Rego, Cedar) if used\n\n**Needs runtime evidence:** That every path to each forbidden operation is actually blocked. Recipe B05: aim each operation at throwaway resources through every tool, API, and shell path, sent straight to enforcement, plus a planted-instruction test for agents with all three capabilities.\n\n**N/A when:** never\n\n## B06 · G1 · C4 — Approval integrity\n#!/usr/bin/env python3\n\"\"\"Write one answer into a findings file, check it, and show what's next.\n\nUse this instead of editing the JSON by hand.\n\n  Answer a check:\n    python answer.py FINDINGS.json check B05.2 --met no --evidence src/x.py:41 \\\\\n        --quote \"exact code on that line\" --note \"what the code does\" [--counter \"what you searched\"]\n\n  Add a red flag:\n    python answer.py FINDINGS.json flag B05 --evidence src/x.py:41 --note \"what's wrong\"\n\n  Finish an item:\n    python answer.py FINDINGS.json item B05 --rating gap --confidence high \\\\\n        --summary \"one sentence\" --next \"what to fix\" [--violates \"exact words from the requirement\"] \\\\\n        [--also-affects E06]\n\n  Resolve a pre-scan hit:\n    python answer.py FINDINGS.json prescan H003 --verdict confirmed --item R03 --reason \"...\"\n\n  Show the next unanswered check:\n    python answer.py FINDINGS.json next\n\nThe quote is checked against the file right away; a \"yes\" without a matching quote\nand a counter-search is refused. Paths are relative to the repo, which defaults to the\nfolder holding FINDINGS.json (set --repo to change it).\n\"\"\"\nimport argparse\nimport json\nimport sys\nfrom pathlib import Path\n\nsys.path.insert(0, str(Path(__file__).resolve().parent))\nfrom validate_findings import quote_found, norm, refs  # noqa: E402\n\nCHECKS_FILE = Path(__file__).resolve().parent.parent / \"references\" / \"checks.json\"\n\n\ndef load(path):\n    return json.loads(Path(path).read_text())\n\n\ndef save(path, doc):\n    Path(path).write_text(json.dumps(doc, indent=2) + \"\\n\")\n\n\ndef find_item(doc, item):\n    for f in doc[\"findings\"]:\n        if f[\"item\"] == item:\n            return f\n    sys.exit(f\"{item} isn't in this findings file.\")\n\n\ndef next_open(doc):\n    for f in doc[\"findings\"]:\n        for c in f.get(\"checks\", []):\n            if c.get(\"met\") == \"unknown\" and not norm(c.get(\"note\", \"\")):\n                return f\"Next: {c['id']} — {c.get('check', '')}\\n  How to verify: {c.get('verify', '')}\"\n        if str(f.get(\"summary\", \"\")).startswith(\"TODO\"):\n            return f\"Next: finish item {f['item']} with `answer.py ... item {f['item']} --rating ...`\"","stderr":"","interrupted":false,"isImage":false,"noOutputExpected":false}}
