{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01DSD5F2f6aVeJJuj4vp6C6c","type":"tool_result","content":"## A01 G1 Distinct agent identity | NA: never\nREQ: Give the agent its own non-human identity. Keep it separate from the user who launched it and from unrelated agents. Show that permissions and revocation belong to that identity, even when a user starts the run.\n- A01.1 The agent runs under its own workload principal (service account, IAM role, SPIFFE ID, or agent ID) that no other service or unrelated agent uses.\n- A01.2 Tool and API clients get credentials from the agent's own principal, not from the launching user's forwarded token or session cookie.\n- A01.3 When a user starts a run, the user is carried as a delegated subject (for example a token exchange with separate act and sub), while the agent keeps its own principal.\n- A01.4 Permissions are bound to the agent principal in IaC or RBAC, so revoking that principal alone removes the agent's access without disabling the user.\n## A02 G1 All six identity fields | NA: never\nREQ: Check that every action records the accountable party, operational owner, tenant, agent-type-id, agent-instance-id, and trace context. Look at successful, denied, failed, and background actions, not just the main request path.\n- A02.1 The audit record schema has all six fields: accountable party, operational owner, tenant, agent-type-id, agent-instance-id, and trace context.\n- A02.2 All six fields are written on denied, failed, async, and background actions too, not only on the successful main request path.\n- A02.3 Trace context (trace and span IDs) is stored in the audit record itself, not only in separate tracing spans.\n- A02.4 Tenant and owner values come from the trusted layer, not from baggage, tracestate, or other unprotected headers.\n## A03 G1 Content-derived type identity | NA: never\nREQ: Compute the agent-type-id as a hash of the container digest, harness, system prompt, model ID and version, and configuration. The model part includes checkpoint and fine-tune or adapter references where they apply. Keep the list of inputs (the manifest) and the hashing steps. Check that changing any input changes the ID, and that the same manifest gives the same ID.\n- A03.1 The agent-type-id is a hash over a manifest that includes the container digest, harness, system prompt, model ID and version, and configuration.\n- A03.2 The hash covers every file that defines the prompt, tools, and configuration, with no file or directory skipped or excluded.\n- A03.3 The model input is a pinned version or snapshot (plus checkpoint and adapter references where used), not an alias and not the app's release number.\n- A03.4 The manifest is put in a fixed, documented order before hashing, so the same manifest always gives the same ID.\n- A03.5 Secret values are not placed in the hashed manifest in a form that exposes them.\n## A04 G1 Trustworthy attribution | NA: never\nREQ: Assign instance and tenant identity in the trusted execution layer. Don't accept labels the model supplies. Record the delegated subject — the user or system whose authority is being used — separately from the agent's identity. Test that the agent can't pose as another instance or tenant in requests or audit records. Test that trace context survives handoffs between async jobs. Treat trace IDs and caller-supplied labels only as a way to link records. They never prove identity or permission.\n- A04.1 Tenant and instance identity are set by the harness or platform from verified context, never from model-produced text or tool arguments.\n- A04.2 Tenant or instance labels in tool arguments or agent-controlled headers are rejected or overwritten with the verified values before they are used or logged.\n- A04.3 The delegated subject is recorded separately from the agent identity.\n- A04.4 When work is handed to an async job or queue, the trusted layer attaches the verified attribution and trace context, and the job uses them.\n- A04.5 Authorization never treats trace IDs or caller-supplied labels as proof of identity or permission.\n## A05 G1 Operational lookup | NA: never\nREQ: Start from one suspicious action. Find the team responsible, the current operator, the deployment manifest, and the live instance. Show that you can contain the right instance or agent type without cutting off the launching user's other access.\n- A05.1 Each audit record carries the agent-type-id and agent-instance-id, which resolve to a stored deployment manifest and a live instance.\n- A05.2 An ownership record in code or config maps each agent type to a responsible team and a current operator or on-call rotation.\n- A05.3 A containment control can disable a single instance or a single agent type (for example revoke its principal or scale it to zero) without touching the launching user's credentials.\n## A06 G3 Parent and prompt provenance | NA: The agent never starts sub-agents (no child agents, handoffs, or delegated agent tasks).\nREQ: For every sub-agent, record its own type and instance IDs, a link to its parent, trace links, and the prompt the parent gave it. Trace a nested action back to that prompt. Protect sensitive prompt text with restricted storage and a clear redaction policy. Standard GenAI instrumentation records prompts only when you turn that on, so confirm it is on for this path.\n- A06.1 Each sub-agent gets its own type ID and instance ID, assigned by the harness when it is started.\n- A06.2 Each sub-agent's records include its direct parent's instance ID and a trace link to the parent.\n- A06.3 The prompt the parent passed to the child is captured on this path, with prompt capture switched on in config rather than left at the default off.\n- A06.4 Captured prompt text is stored with restricted access and a redaction policy, and only an approved audit role can read it.\n## A07 G3 Every model in the loop | NA: The agent uses no helper models at all (no verifiers, judges, rerankers, embedding models, or world models), and the model inventory shows that.\nREQ: List all helper models, such as verifiers, judges, rerankers, and world models. Give each one an identity based on its available model version or digest, its prompt, and its configuration, and record which decisions it made. Show that a swapped, drifting, or failing checker counts as a failure of the control that depends on it.\n- A07.1 A model inventory in code or config lists every helper model the agent calls (verifiers, judges, rerankers, embedding models, world models).\n- A07.2 Each helper model is identified by a pinned version or digest plus its prompt and configuration, not by an alias.\n- A07.3 Each helper model's decisions are logged with that model's identity.\n- A07.4 A checker timeout, error, invalid output, or version mismatch makes the control that depends on it fail (block or escalate), not pass by default.\n## R01 G2 Validate every input boundary, including API responses | NA: never\nREQ: List every input the agent receives. That includes API responses (errors and streamed content too), tool and connector output, incoming webhooks and callbacks, messages from other agents, user input, fetched pages, files, and retrieved chunks. Authenticate each sender (prove who sent it), and verify signatures where they exist. Treat content as untrusted even when it comes from an authenticated service or an internal one. Before content reaches the model or runs anywhere, check its type, schema, and size. Also check its content for attack payloads and hidden prompt-injection instructions. Reject or hold aside bad payloads. If a required check fails to run, don't let unchecked content through. Test broken, oversized, faked, and hostile responses from each integration.\n- R01.1 Every input path passes through validation code: API and tool responses (including errors and streams), webhooks and callbacks, agent messages, user input, fetched pages, files, and retrieved chunks.\n- R01.2 Type, schema, and size checks run before content, including streamed chunks, is added to the model context or executed.\n- R01.3 Senders are authenticated, and signatures are verified where the integration provides them, including for internal and authenticated services.\n- R01.4 Content is also screened for attack payloads or injection instructions, not only for structure.\n- R01.5 Bad payloads are rejected or held aside, and if a check errors, times out, or is skipped, the content is blocked.\n## R02 G2 Separate data from instructions and test injection containment | NA: never\nREQ: Keep API responses, connector messages, and other outside content out of trusted system and policy channels. Label where each piece came from and how much it is trusted. Test prompt injection in responses that pass schema checks. Include free-text fields, error messages, documents, and tool output. Checks and channel separation can't guarantee that prompt injection is prevented. So show that credential limits, tool permissions, and destructive-action gates still block harm if the model follows an injected instruction. Repeat each attack and include adaptive versions. One failed attempt makes a defense look stronger than it is. B05 covers agents that combine untrusted input, sensitive access, and outside actions.\n- R02.1 Outside content (API responses, tool output, documents, connector messages) goes into user or tool message roles, never into the system or policy prompt.\n- R02.2 Each piece of outside content carries a source and trust label in the model request.\n- R02.3 Credential limits, tool permissions, and B05 action gates block the actions an injected instruction could cause, enforced in code the model cannot change.\n- R02.4 Containment does not rely on prompt wording, delimiters, or an injection detector.\n- R02.5 Injection tests repeat each fixture a set number of times, include adaptive variants and schema-valid payloads, and report success rates.\n## R03 G2 Prevent output and log leakage | NA: never\nREQ: Remove secrets and limit sensitive data before tool output, debug output, or responses reach the model, outside recipients, or normal logs. Some clients fetch URLs on their own when they display output, such as Markdown images and link previews. These can leak data without any tool call, so block them or allow only approved URLs. Check generated output before it goes into SQL, a shell, an HTML page, or anything else that runs it. Use parameterized queries and commands (for example, argument lists for shell calls), and the right escaping for each context. Test with fake secrets and injection payloads. Confirm that leaks and unintended execution are blocked, and that redacted audit evidence is kept.\n- R03.1 Secrets and sensitive fields are redacted from tool output, debug output, and error text before they reach the model, outside recipients, or normal logs.\n- R03.2 The display client blocks auto-fetched URLs (Markdown images, link previews) or allows only approved hosts.\n- R03.3 Generated SQL uses parameterized queries, shell calls use argument lists with no string-built commands, and HTML output is escaped for its context.\n- R03.4 Redacted audit evidence is kept rather than discarded.\n## R04 G2 Retrieval-corpus integrity | NA: Only when the agent has no retrieval corpus or search index.\nREQ: The retrieval corpus is the set of documents the agent searches for context. Its index stores those documents, or smaller passages called chunks, in a search engine or vector database. Limit which identities and sources can add or change content there. For each chunk, keep its source, when it was added, its trust level, and its access permissions. Enforce the requesting tenant's access when searching. Test an unapproved attempt to add content and a search across tenants. Also test quarantining, expiring, and revoking access to seeded test documents. Confirm the affected chunks disappear from search results and caches within a stated time. See the [R04 recipe](CHECKLIST-VERIFICATION.md#r--run-time).\n- R04.1 Only named identities and sources can add or change index content, and the ingestion path checks the writer.\n- R04.2 Each stored chunk has metadata for source, time added, trust level, and access permissions.\n- R04.3 Search applies the requesting tenant's access filter at query time, in the store or retrieval code, not after the model answers.\n- R04.4 Code can quarantine, expire, and revoke chunks, and it also clears or invalidates search caches.\n## R05 G3 Memory isolation and write validation | NA: Only when the agent has no memory that lasts between runs.\nREQ: Separate memory by tenant, agent type, instance, and task as needed. Block access across those lines unless it is clearly approved. Check what gets written to memory. Test that one poisoned run can't quietly plant instructions in another run's memory. Don't move the agent's own output into trusted memory without review, and let unverified entries expire. Write checks must also cover content that arrives through ordinary queries, not just special write paths.\n- R05.1 Memory reads and writes are scoped by tenant, agent type, instance, and task as needed, and cross-scope access is denied unless explicitly allowed.\n- R05.2 Every memory write, including content arriving through ordinary queries, passes validation before it is stored.\n- R05.3 The agent's own output cannot reach trusted or shared memory without a review step.\n- R05.4 Unverified entries have an expiry that is enforced.\n## R06 G3 Memory provenance and cleanup | NA: Only when the agent has no memory that lasts between runs.\nREQ: For any memory entry, look up who wrote it, when, for which task, from what input, and the context size at the time. Show that you can find, quarantine, and remove bad entries, and control their reuse in later sessions.\n- R06.1 Each memory entry stores its writer, time, task, source input, and context size at write time.\n- R06.2 Derived entries such as summaries keep links to the entries they came from.\n- R06.3 Code can find entries by source or writer and quarantine or delete them, along with derived entries and caches.\n- R06.4 Quarantined or deleted entries are excluded from retrieval in later sessions.\n## R07 G2 Decision-time context size | NA: never\nREQ: For each action, log how full the model's context was when it decided, in tokens. Document how you count. Say whether each value was measured, estimated, or unavailable. Cover cached input, compaction (shrinking the context), and limits the provider hides. Per-call billing totals don't automatically equal context size. Use them only if your counting method shows they match. A missing value is a clear gap, never zero. Check that the value survives tool handoffs and can be searched next to the action's identity and result. See the [telemetry conventions](otel-conventions.md).\n- R07.1 Each action record includes the model's context size in tokens at decision time.\n- R07.2 Each value is labeled measured, estimated, or unavailable, and a missing value is stored as unknown, never as zero.\n- R07.3 The count comes from a stated method that handles cached input and compaction, not a copied per-call billing total.\n- R07.4 The value is carried across tool handoffs and stored with the action's identity, result, and trace ID.\n## R08 G3 Security monitoring | NA: never\nREQ: Keep security rules and baselines separate from answer-quality monitoring. Test encoded payloads, unexpected outbound traffic, writes outside scope, privilege escalation, and repeated denied calls. Check that alerts reach a named responder.\n- R08.1 Security detection rules and baselines are defined separately from answer-quality monitoring.\n- R08.2 Rules exist for encoded payloads, unexpected outbound traffic, out-of-scope writes, privilege escalation, and repeated denied calls.\n- R08.3 Alerts from these rules route to a named responder or on-call target in config.\n## R09 G3 Sequence and context-aware detection | NA: never\nREQ: Test suspicious chains of actions that are each allowed on their own. Also test unusual fan-out and resource use. Compare behavior across context-size ranges, so one fleet-wide baseline doesn't hide failures in long contexts. If the agent could be hijacked while using only allowed tools, check each high-impact action against the original task before it runs. Use a checker that doesn't see the untrusted content in between, and measure what it misses.\n- R09.1 Detection rules evaluate sequences of calls across a run (for example many reads followed by an outside send), not only single calls.\n- R09.2 Rules cover unusual fan-out and resource use.\n- R09.3 Thresholds or baselines are set per context-size range, not as one fleet-wide value.\n- R09.4 If hijack through allowed tools alone is possible, each high-impact action is checked against the original task before it runs, by a checker that is not given the untrusted content.\n## R10 G3 Misaligned-behavior review | NA: Only when deliberate misalignment is recorded as out of scope for this deployment, with a reason and approval.\nREQ: If deliberate misalignment is in scope, test a second model or pattern review against realistic evasive behavior. Record what it can and can't catch. Include what the reviewer can see: actions, arguments, and any visible reasoning. Reasoning the reviewer can't see is a stated gap. Include red-team runs by an agent that knows it is being watched. This review must not replace rule-based access and action controls that don't depend on a model's judgment. Deeper checks on intent belong in a separate AI-control program at the model level.\n## R11 G1 Tested kill switch | NA: never\nREQ: Name who may stop the agent and how. The stop path must not depend on the agent's cooperation. The agent's credentials must not be able to change it. Set a maximum stop time that fits the potential harm. Run a dated drill on the current deployment. It must stop new actions, running work, and open tool streams, subscriptions, and tasks. Include an attempt by the agent to disable or delay the stop. Cover sub-agents and their children in the ecosystem drill (E09).\n- R11.1 A stop control halts new model calls and new tool calls when set.\n- R11.2 The agent's own credentials and tools cannot set, clear, delete, or delay the stop control.\n- R11.3 Tool calls already running are cancelled or bounded by a timeout when the stop is set.\n- R11.4 The stop also closes open tool streams, subscriptions, and background tasks.\n- R11.5 Only named operator identities can set the stop control.\n## R12 G1 Safe state after stopping | NA: never\nREQ: Decide what happens to writes in progress, queued actions, and outside side effects. Where you can't cancel something, show how you check and fix the result (reconcile) or undo it another way (compensate). No restart may happen unattended, and no orphaned work may be left able to act.\n- R12.1 For each operation type, code or config states whether it can be cancelled, completed, or only reconciled after a stop.\n- R12.2 On stop, queued actions are cancelled or held, and automatic retries are turned off.\n- R12.3 Unclear or in-progress outside actions go on a tracked hold with an owner, and dependent actions are blocked until reconciled.\n- R12.4 A stopped run does not restart on its own; restart needs an operator action.\n- R12.5 Workers and queued jobs lose their credentials or authority at stop, so no orphaned work can act.\n## R13 G1 Full execution audit | NA: never\nREQ: Record each run as an execution graph: a linked record of every step. It must include proposed actions, tool calls and key arguments, and authorization decisions with the policy version used. It must also include denials, approvals with their IDs, results, errors, and timestamps. Rebuild a typical run from this record, including failed actions. Don't rely on the run's final answer.\n- R13.1 Each step writes a record linked to the run: proposed action, tool call with key arguments, result, error, and timestamp.\n- R13.2 Authorization decisions are recorded with the policy version, including denials, not only allowed calls.\n- R13.3 Approved actions record their approval ID.\n- R13.4 Tool errors, timeouts, and retries are recorded, and each operation gets an ID before it is sent, so unclear results can be matched to the destination's records.\n- R13.5 Records are written by the harness or gateway, not by the model, and do not depend on the run's final answer.\n## R14 G2 Recovery integrity | NA: Only when no logs or checkpoints drive recovery and there is no automatic replay.\nREQ: If logs or checkpoints drive recovery, make them tamper-evident. Before using them, verify them against a separately protected trust anchor or signing key. Test that verification rejects changed checkpoints. It must also catch truncation, rollback to an older valid checkpoint, and replacement of a whole hash chain. For automatic recovery, confirm the destination supports idempotency: sending the same request twice has the effect of sending it once. Replay only within the destination's documented scope and time window. Confirm that actions you can't undo don't happen twice. Where support is missing or the result is unclear, check that replay pauses for reconciliation instead of sending again.\n- R14.1 Checkpoints and audit records used for recovery are hash-chained or signed.\n- R14.2 Recovery verifies them against a key or trust anchor stored separately from the records, and refuses any that fail.\n- R14.3 Verification catches truncation, rollback to an older valid checkpoint, and replacement of the whole chain, for example with a signed head or sequence number held outside the store.\n- R14.4 Automatic replay sends idempotency keys that the destination enforces, and only for integrations that support them.\n- R14.5 When idempotency is missing or the result is unclear, replay pauses for reconciliation instead of resending.","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"7b08f688-90d5-4b4c-af7c-eac511a967ff","uuid":"4cbabbb8-8c50-4244-81ee-14e00bef0aca","timestamp":"2026-10-02T14:24:39.119Z","tool_use_result":{"stdout":"## A01 G1 Distinct agent identity | NA: never\nREQ: Give the agent its own non-human identity. Keep it separate from the user who launched it and from unrelated agents. Show that permissions and revocation belong to that identity, even when a user starts the run.\n- A01.1 The agent runs under its own workload principal (service account, IAM role, SPIFFE ID, or agent ID) that no other service or unrelated agent uses.\n- A01.2 Tool and API clients get credentials from the agent's own principal, not from the launching user's forwarded token or session cookie.\n- A01.3 When a user starts a run, the user is carried as a delegated subject (for example a token exchange with separate act and sub), while the agent keeps its own principal.\n- A01.4 Permissions are bound to the agent principal in IaC or RBAC, so revoking that principal alone removes the agent's access without disabling the user.\n## A02 G1 All six identity fields | NA: never\nREQ: Check that every action records the accountable party, operational owner, tenant, agent-type-id, agent-instance-id, and trace context. Look at successful, denied, failed, and background actions, not just the main request path.\n- A02.1 The audit record schema has all six fields: accountable party, operational owner, tenant, agent-type-id, agent-instance-id, and trace context.\n- A02.2 All six fields are written on denied, failed, async, and background actions too, not only on the successful main request path.\n- A02.3 Trace context (trace and span IDs) is stored in the audit record itself, not only in separate tracing spans.\n- A02.4 Tenant and owner values come from the trusted layer, not from baggage, tracestate, or other unprotected headers.\n## A03 G1 Content-derived type identity | NA: never\nREQ: Compute the agent-type-id as a hash of the container digest, harness, system prompt, model ID and version, and configuration. The model part includes checkpoint and fine-tune or adapter references where they apply. Keep the list of inputs (the manifest) and the hashing steps. Check that changing any input changes the ID, and that the same manifest gives the same ID.\n- A03.1 The agent-type-id is a hash over a manifest that includes the container digest, harness, system prompt, model ID and version, and configuration.\n- A03.2 The hash covers every file that defines the prompt, tools, and configuration, with no file or directory skipped or excluded.\n- A03.3 The model input is a pinned version or snapshot (plus checkpoint and adapter references where used), not an alias and not the app's release number.\n- A03.4 The manifest is put in a fixed, documented order before hashing, so the same manifest always gives the same ID.\n- A03.5 Secret values are not placed in the hashed manifest in a form that exposes them.\n## A04 G1 Trustworthy attribution | NA: never\nREQ: Assign instance and tenant identity in the trusted execution layer. Don't accept labels the model supplies. Record the delegated subject — the user or system whose authority is being used — separately from the agent's identity. Test that the agent can't pose as another instance or tenant in requests or audit records. Test that trace context survives handoffs between async jobs. Treat trace IDs and caller-supplied labels only as a way to link records. They never prove identity or permission.\n- A04.1 Tenant and instance identity are set by the harness or platform from verified context, never from model-produced text or tool arguments.\n- A04.2 Tenant or instance labels in tool arguments or agent-controlled headers are rejected or overwritten with the verified values before they are used or logged.\n- A04.3 The delegated subject is recorded separately from the agent identity.\n- A04.4 When work is handed to an async job or queue, the trusted layer attaches the verified attribution and trace context, and the job uses them.\n- A04.5 Authorization never treats trace IDs or caller-supplied labels as proof of identity or permission.\n## A05 G1 Operational lookup | NA: never\nREQ: Start from one suspicious action. Find the team responsible, the current operator, the deployment manifest, and the live instance. Show that you can contain the right instance or agent type without cutting off the launching user's other access.\n- A05.1 Each audit record carries the agent-type-id and agent-instance-id, which resolve to a stored deployment manifest and a live instance.\n- A05.2 An ownership record in code or config maps each agent type to a responsible team and a current operator or on-call rotation.\n- A05.3 A containment control can disable a single instance or a single agent type (for example revoke its principal or scale it to zero) without touching the launching user's credentials.\n## A06 G3 Parent and prompt provenance | NA: The agent never starts sub-agents (no child agents, handoffs, or delegated agent tasks).\nREQ: For every sub-agent, record its own type and instance IDs, a link to its parent, trace links, and the prompt the parent gave it. Trace a nested action back to that prompt. Protect sensitive prompt text with restricted storage and a clear redaction policy. Standard GenAI instrumentation records prompts only when you turn that on, so confirm it is on for this path.\n- A06.1 Each sub-agent gets its own type ID and instance ID, assigned by the harness when it is started.\n- A06.2 Each sub-agent's records include its direct parent's instance ID and a trace link to the parent.\n- A06.3 The prompt the parent passed to the child is captured on this path, with prompt capture switched on in config rather than left at the default off.\n- A06.4 Captured prompt text is stored with restricted access and a redaction policy, and only an approved audit role can read it.\n## A07 G3 Every model in the loop | NA: The agent uses no helper models at all (no verifiers, judges, rerankers, embedding models, or world models), and the model inventory shows that.\nREQ: List all helper models, such as verifiers, judges, rerankers, and world models. Give each one an identity based on its available model version or digest, its prompt, and its configuration, and record which decisions it made. Show that a swapped, drifting, or failing checker counts as a failure of the control that depends on it.\n- A07.1 A model inventory in code or config lists every helper model the agent calls (verifiers, judges, rerankers, embedding models, world models).\n- A07.2 Each helper model is identified by a pinned version or digest plus its prompt and configuration, not by an alias.\n- A07.3 Each helper model's decisions are logged with that model's identity.\n- A07.4 A checker timeout, error, invalid output, or version mismatch makes the control that depends on it fail (block or escalate), not pass by default.\n## R01 G2 Validate every input boundary, including API responses | NA: never\nREQ: List every input the agent receives. That includes API responses (errors and streamed content too), tool and connector output, incoming webhooks and callbacks, messages from other agents, user input, fetched pages, files, and retrieved chunks. Authenticate each sender (prove who sent it), and verify signatures where they exist. Treat content as untrusted even when it comes from an authenticated service or an internal one. Before content reaches the model or runs anywhere, check its type, schema, and size. Also check its content for attack payloads and hidden prompt-injection instructions. Reject or hold aside bad payloads. If a required check fails to run, don't let unchecked content through. Test broken, oversized, faked, and hostile responses from each integration.\n- R01.1 Every input path passes through validation code: API and tool responses (including errors and streams), webhooks and callbacks, agent messages, user input, fetched pages, files, and retrieved chunks.\n- R01.2 Type, schema, and size checks run before content, including streamed chunks, is added to the model context or executed.\n- R01.3 Senders are authenticated, and signatures are verified where the integration provides them, including for internal and authenticated services.\n- R01.4 Content is also screened for attack payloads or injection instructions, not only for structure.\n- R01.5 Bad payloads are rejected or held aside, and if a check errors, times out, or is skipped, the content is blocked.\n## R02 G2 Separate data from instructions and test injection containment | NA: never\nREQ: Keep API responses, connector messages, and other outside content out of trusted system and policy channels. Label where each piece came from and how much it is trusted. Test prompt injection in responses that pass schema checks. Include free-text fields, error messages, documents, and tool output. Checks and channel separation can't guarantee that prompt injection is prevented. So show that credential limits, tool permissions, and destructive-action gates still block harm if the model follows an injected instruction. Repeat each attack and include adaptive versions. One failed attempt makes a defense look stronger than it is. B05 covers agents that combine untrusted input, sensitive access, and outside actions.\n- R02.1 Outside content (API responses, tool output, documents, connector messages) goes into user or tool message roles, never into the system or policy prompt.\n- R02.2 Each piece of outside content carries a source and trust label in the model request.\n- R02.3 Credential limits, tool permissions, and B05 action gates block the actions an injected instruction could cause, enforced in code the model cannot change.\n- R02.4 Containment does not rely on prompt wording, delimiters, or an injection detector.\n- R02.5 Injection tests repeat each fixture a set number of times, include adaptive variants and schema-valid payloads, and report success rates.\n## R03 G2 Prevent output and log leakage | NA: never\nREQ: Remove secrets and limit sensitive data before tool output, debug output, or responses reach the model, outside recipients, or normal logs. Some clients fetch URLs on their own when they display output, such as Markdown images and link previews. These can leak data without any tool call, so block them or allow only approved URLs. Check generated output before it goes into SQL, a shell, an HTML page, or anything else that runs it. Use parameterized queries and commands (for example, argument lists for shell calls), and the right escaping for each context. Test with fake secrets and injection payloads. Confirm that leaks and unintended execution are blocked, and that redacted audit evidence is kept.\n- R03.1 Secrets and sensitive fields are redacted from tool output, debug output, and error text before they reach the model, outside recipients, or normal logs.\n- R03.2 The display client blocks auto-fetched URLs (Markdown images, link previews) or allows only approved hosts.\n- R03.3 Generated SQL uses parameterized queries, shell calls use argument lists with no string-built commands, and HTML output is escaped for its context.\n- R03.4 Redacted audit evidence is kept rather than discarded.\n## R04 G2 Retrieval-corpus integrity | NA: Only when the agent has no retrieval corpus or search index.\nREQ: The retrieval corpus is the set of documents the agent searches for context. Its index stores those documents, or smaller passages called chunks, in a search engine or vector database. Limit which identities and sources can add or change content there. For each chunk, keep its source, when it was added, its trust level, and its access permissions. Enforce the requesting tenant's access when searching. Test an unapproved attempt to add content and a search across tenants. Also test quarantining, expiring, and revoking access to seeded test documents. Confirm the affe